C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 Fall 2015
      Problem 7.4
       
      A farmer plans to develop water for irrigation. He is considering two possible sources of
      water: a gravity diversion from a possible reservoir with two alternative capacities and/or a
      pump from a lower river diversion (refer to Figure 7.3). Between the reservoir and pump
      site the river base flow increases by 2 acft/day due to groundwater drainage into the river.
      Ignore losses from the reservoir. The river flow into the reservoir and the farmer's demand
      during each of two six-month seasons of the year are given in Table 7.5. Revenue is estimated
      at $300 per year per acre irrigated.
       
      Season, t River Inflow, Qt (acft) Irrigation Demand (acft/acre)
      1 600 1.0
      2 200 3.0
       
      Assume that there are only two possible sizes of reservoir: a high dam that has capacity of
      700 acft or a low dam with capacity of 300 acft. The capital costs are $10,000/year and
      $6,000/year for the high and low dams, respectively (no operating cost). The pump capacity
      is fixed at 2.2 acft/day with a capital cost (if it is built) of $8,000/year and operating cost
      of $20/acft.
       
      Integer version using INTEGER VARIABLES statement and SOLVE as MIP.
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 28, 2015
  29   
  30  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  31  SETS src water supply sources /rh "reservoir high", rl "reservoir low",p "pump"/
  32      time year /y1 "Season 1", y2 "Season 2"/;
  33   
  34  * 2. DEFINE input data
  35  PARAMETERS
  36     CapCost(src) capital cost ($ to build)
  37           /rh 10000,
  38            rl 6000,
  39            p 8000/
  40     OpCost(src) operating cost ($ per ac-ft)
  41           /rh 0,
  42            rl 0,
  43            p 20/
  44     MaxCapacity(src) Maximum capacity of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  45           /rh 700,
  46            rl 300,
  47            p 396/
  48     Inflow(time) Inflow every season
  49            /y1 600,
  50             y2  200/
  51     Irrigation(time) Inflow every year
  52            /y1 1,
  53             y2  3/
  54   
  55      GW  groundwater gain /360/
  56  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  57  VARIABLES I(src) binary decision to build or do prject from source src (1=yes 0=no)
  58            X(src,time) volume of water provided by source src (ac-ft per year)
  59            AC acre to be irrigated
  60            Spill(time) water flow from reservoir to pump per season
  61            S(time) carryover storage reservoir
  62            TCOST  Maximum Benfiits-Cost ($);
  63   
  64   
  65  BINARY VARIABLES I;
  66  * Non-negativity constraints
  67  POSITIVE VARIABLES X,AC,Spill,S;
  68   
  69  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  70  EQUATIONS
  71     COST            Total Cost ($) and objective function value
  72     massbal(time) reservoir mass balance
  73     demand(time)  crop water demand
  74     storcap(time) storage limited by reservoir built
  75     relcap_h(time) release only if high reservoir built
  76     relcap_l(time) release only if low reservoir built
  77     pumpcap(time) pump capacity
  78     pumpriver(time) pump limited by river flow
  79     onereservoir        at most one reservoir;
  80   
  81  COST..  TCOST =e= 300*AC
  82                    - sum(src, CapCost(src)*I(src))
  83                    - sum((src,time), OpCost(src)*X(src,time));
  84   
  85  massbal(time)..  S(time++1) =e= S(time) + Inflow(time) - Spill(time)
  86                                  - X('rh',time) - X('rl',time);
  87   
  88  demand(time)..   sum(src, X(src,time)) =g= Irrigation(time)*AC;
  89   
  90  storcap(time)..  S(time) =l= MaxCapacity('rh')*I('rh') + MaxCapacity('rl')*I('rl');
  91   
  92  relcap_h(time)..  X('rh',time) =l= (MaxCapacity('rh') + Inflow(time))*I('rh');
  93  relcap_l(time)..  X('rl',time) =l= (MaxCapacity('rl') + Inflow(time))*I('rl');
  94   
  95  pumpcap(time)..  X('p',time) =l= MaxCapacity('p')*I('p');
  96   
  97  pumpriver(time).. X('p',time) =l= Spill(time) + GW;
  98   
  99  onereservoir..   I('rh') + I('rl') =l= 1;
 100   
 101  MODEL Irrig /all/;
 102  SOLVE Irrig USING mip MAXIMIZING TCOST;
 103   
 104  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
 105   
 106   
 107   
 108   
 109  * Dump all input data and results to a GAMS gdx file
 110  *Execute_Unload "Ex6-3-integer.gdx";
